WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.080
Thanks for joining me today. I'm Patrick and Vancouver, and this is Planet Terri and Daily.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.920
We're going to move through some genuinely interesting clinical readouts,

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:11.540
a few corporate moves that tell us where the industry is placing its bets,

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.480
and then we'll spend a little longer on two stories that I think deserve more breathing room.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.620
One about real progress in an almost impossible cancer, and another about the hidden chemistry

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:27.420
that quietly determines whether we can actually build the clean energy future we talk about.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:32.460
Let's get into it. We'll start with oncology because the last few weeks have delivered a couple

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:37.540
of data points that feel quietly significant rather than headline grabbing. First,

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:43.820
IDIA Biosciences and its partner Servier announced that their investigational drug for a rare eye cancer

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:48.900
met the primary endpoint in a phase two-slash three trial. The compound is designed to target

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:55.060
UVO melanoma, a malignancy that starts in the pigment cells of the eye. While UVO melanoma is

00:00:55.060 --> 00:01:00.100
uncommon, it's serious. When it spreads beyond the eye, outcomes have historically been poor.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:06.260
The fact that this targeted agent cleared its efficacy bar opens the door for an accelerated FDA

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:12.320
filing later this year. What I find encouraging here is the precision. Local therapies for

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.940
ocular tumors can sometimes avoid the heavier systemic side effects that come with traditional chemotherapy.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.720
If this drug ultimately gains approval, it could give ophthalmologists and oncologists a more

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:29.320
tailored option for patients whose disease is caught before it metastasizes widely. Of course,

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.720
we'll need to see the full data set, including how durable the responses are and what the safety

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.840
profile looks like in a broader group. But meeting the primary endpoint in a

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.860
registration style study is a meaningful step forward for a rare cancer that hasn't seen many new

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:48.340
tools. Right around the same time, another oncology company delivered news that feels even more striking

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:53.800
because of how stubborn the disease has been. Revolution medicines reported that its PAN-RAS

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:59.660
inhibitor, given in a phase 3 trial for metastatic pancreatic cancer, extended median overall

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:05.220
survival by six months compared with standard chemotherapy. On the surface, that might not sound

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:10.460
enormous, but context matters enormously here. Pancreatic cancer has been one of the toughest

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:16.300
solid tumors to move the needle on. For years, even the best new agents in phase 3 have typically

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:22.320
added only a matter of weeks, not months. This result therefore stands out. The drug is designed to

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:29.080
hit multiple mutations in the RAS family of genes. Those mutations are thought to drive roughly 90%

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:34.920
of pancreatic tumors, which helps explain why a single agent that can address several of them at once

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:40.580
might finally produce a more noticeable clinical benefit. For patients and clinicians dealing with

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:46.040
metastatic disease, six months of additional median survival is one of the more substantial gains we've

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:52.040
seen in a long time. It doesn't turn pancreatic cancer into an easy disease, but it does suggest the

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:58.440
treatment landscape could be shifting. That said, important questions remain. We'll want to understand

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:03.500
how durable those survival gains prove to be once the data mature further and whether the benefit

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:08.720
holds up across more diverse patient populations. Regulators will also have to weigh the high

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:14.340
unmet need against the absolute magnitude of benefit. Still, the result feels like one of those

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:19.500
incremental but hard-won victories that can quietly change practice if the full package looks clean.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:24.640
It's the kind of data that reminds me why we keep grinding away at these incredibly difficult

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:30.120
molecular problems. While one company makes progress against an intractable solid tumor,

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:36.200
another is testing a very different idea in blood cancer. Allogene therapeutic shared early data on

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:42.860
its Allogeneic CAR-T therapy known as CMACEL in patients with lymphoma. In the study, the off-the-shelf

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:47.820
product eliminated minimal residual disease in seven out of twelve patients. By comparison,

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:52.660
only two out of twelve patients in the observational control arm reached the same milestone. The numbers

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:57.860
are small, which is important to keep in mind, but the direction is encouraging. Allogeneic or ready-made

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:01.760
CAR-T therapies have been something of a holy grail because they could remove the weeks-long

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.920
manufacturing delays and logistical headaches that come with autologous products made from a patient's

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:12.680
own cells. If an off-the-shelf approach can reliably mop up lingering cancer cells after

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:17.360
initial treatment, it could become a practical tool in the broader blood cancer toolkit.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:24.120
The early signal that CMACEL can do this without excessive toxicity is worth following closely as

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:29.200
the program advances. It's interesting to see both autologous and allogeneic cell therapies

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:35.120
continuing to evolve side-by-side, each has strengths and trade-offs. The fact that allogeneic products are

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:40.680
now showing this level of biological activity suggests the field may be getting closer to making

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:46.580
them a standard part of care rather than an experimental curiosity. We'll see how the data look

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:51.900
in larger cohorts, but the early read feels like another brick in the wall of evidence that ready-made

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:57.760
cell therapies can work. These clinical wins sit against a broader backdrop of shifting corporate

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:04.040
strategies. Big players continue to acquire assets that fit specific portfolio gaps while smaller

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:10.020
innovators push their own candidates forward. ABBV, for example, agreed to pay $30 million up front

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:15.160
to China's high-scope pharmaceutical for two acute pain candidates with the full deal potentially

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:22.120
reaching $745 million in milestones. The move makes strategic sense at a moment when non-opioid pain

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:27.000
relief remains a major unmet need and when regulators and physicians are still looking for alternatives that

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:32.180
don't carry addiction risks. It's another data point showing that large pharma is willing to pay for

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:37.520
assets that could offer a cleaner safety profile in the pain space. On the smaller biotech side,

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:42.640
Spire Therapeutics announced that its monoclonal antibody hit the primary endpoint in a phase two

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:48.080
study of ulcerative colitis. The company is openly positioning the drug as a potential future

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:53.560
competitor to Takeda's antivio, which has been a workhorse in inflammatory bowel disease for years.

00:05:54.280 --> 00:06:00.340
Positive phase two data in UC are always welcome, and this result adds yet another contender to an

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:05.820
already growing list of options for patients with IBD. The real test will come in later trials.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:10.880
Can this candidate differentiate itself on efficacy, dosing convenience, or safety?

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:15.580
That's what will ultimately decide whether it becomes a meaningful player or simply another

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:22.380
entrant in a crowded field. Not every story this week is about success. The FDA recently reminded

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:26.700
the entire industry that transparency and clinical research still matters a great deal.

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:33.260
An agency review found that results remain unreported for about 30% of registered clinical studies.

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:39.080
In response, the FDA sent reminders to roughly 2,200 drug companies,

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:44.680
device makers, and individual researchers, urging them to post their data as required.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:50.360
It's a quiet but important signal. Greater transparency protects patients, allows physicians

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:55.200
to make better decisions, and ultimately helps the scientific community move faster by learning

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:00.780
from both positive and negative results. We've come a long way on trial registration, but compliance

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:05.480
clearly isn't perfect yet. Moves like this help keep the pressure on.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:11.280
While regulators push for better reporting, investors continue to fund platforms they believe

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:17.720
can change detection or measurement in meaningful ways. Harbinger Health closed a $100 million

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:23.800
financing round to advance its blood-based multi-cancer early detection tests. The money coincides

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:28.960
with the launch of the company's latest resolve platform. Harbinger is part of a growing wave

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.700
of liquid biopsy technologies that aim to spot cancers earlier through a simple blood draw

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:38.900
rather than waiting for symptoms or relying on imaging alone. If these tests can improve early

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:43.660
detection rates without generating too many false positives that lead to unnecessary procedures,

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:49.500
they could meaningfully shift outcomes for several tumor types. It's still early days for the field

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:54.680
as a whole, but the continued inflow of capital suggests investors see real potential.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:59.520
Some companies are also testing whether the public markets are receptive again.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:04.960
Alamar Biosciences, a California company focused on high-resolution protein sequencing,

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:12.120
has filed to raise up to $159 million in an initial public offering. The filing reflects a broader,

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:17.360
if cautious, reopening of life sciences IPO markets after a difficult couple of years.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:22.280
Alamar's technology is interesting because it could give researchers a sharper,

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:27.460
more quantitative look at proteins, something that has historically been harder to do at scale

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:33.460
than sequencing DNA or RNA. Better protein measurements could improve both basic science

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:38.720
and eventually diagnostic and therapeutic development. We'll see how the offering is received,

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:43.820
but the mere fact that it's moving forward is worth noting. The MedTech sector is seeing its own

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:50.400
form of consolidation. Striker has acquired amplitude vascular systems while Avanos medical is

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:55.460
preparing to go private. These deals illustrate the continued trend of larger players,

00:08:55.740 --> 00:09:00.720
absorbing specialized technologies as the industry adjusts to new economic pressures and tries to

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.940
build more comprehensive portfolios. At the same time, not every corporate move is about expansion.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:11.620
Takeda has terminated a partnership with a Japanese firm working on small molecules that target

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:18.420
messenger RNA, reflecting shifting research priorities. Separately, Estella's announced it will close

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:24.700
its stem cell therapy facility in Seattle, affecting about 55 employees. These decisions are never

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:29.600
easy, but they show how companies continually rebalance pipelines and spending in response to both

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:35.740
scientific data and economic realities. Portfolio pruning is simply part of the rhythm of biotech

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:41.100
and pharma. Now, let's spend a little more time on what makes that pancreatic cancer result from

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:45.180
revolution medicines particularly noteworthy, because I think it's easy to understate its importance

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:50.020
if you're not steeped in the history of this disease. Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:54.940
has one of the lowest five-year survival rates of any major cancer. For decades, even modest

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:59.820
improvements in median survival were measured in weeks rather than months. So when a phase 3 trial

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:04.720
reports a six-month extension in median overall survival, it deserves to be taken seriously.

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:09.820
The pan-rass inhibitor appears to be doing something that earlier agents struggled to achieve,

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:15.200
hitting multiple different RIS mutations that collectively drive the vast majority of these tumors.

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:21.760
That breadth of coverage is likely part of why the benefit looks more substantial than what we've seen

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:26.480
before. For patients with metastatic disease, this could translate into months of additional time

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:31.720
with their families, more time to benefit from other supportive therapies, and perhaps even a bridge

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:36.920
to future options that aren't available today. It's not a cure, and it won't transform every case,

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:42.300
but in a disease this recalcitrant, six months of median survival gain is one of the more

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:47.160
meaningful phase 3 readouts we've seen in recent years. Of course, the next chapters matter.

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:51.840
We need to understand how durable the responses are in the tale of the survival curve,

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:57.940
whether the benefit is consistent across different patient subgroups, and how regulators ultimately view

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:04.300
the risk-benefit profile given the high unmet need. Those discussions will play out over the coming

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:09.820
months, but even at this stage, the result feels like tangible progress against one of oncology's

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:15.380
longest-standing frustrations. It's the kind of incremental win that, when added to others,

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:19.760
can slowly change the conversation from nothing works to we're finally gaining ground.

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:25.220
Before we wrap up, I want to shift gears completely and talk about something most of us get wrong

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:29.980
when we think about the materials that make modern life possible. Specifically, the copper in our

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:36.260
phones, the wiring and wind turbines, the motors and electric vehicles. Most of us picture giant open

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:41.040
pit mines and smoky smelters and assume the metal is simply dug out of the ground and melted.

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:47.720
The reality is more complicated and more interesting. Roughly 70 percent of the world's copper comes from

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:52.480
a mineral called chalcopyright. Its crystal structure is stubbornly resistant to conventional

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:58.780
extraction methods. That recalcitrant forces miners to use energy-intensive processes that generate

00:11:58.780 --> 00:12:04.600
considerable waste and require furnaces running at extremely high temperatures, sometimes above 1,000

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:10.600
degrees Celsius. As global demand for copper surges to support electrification, finding cleaner,

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:15.480
more efficient ways to liberate copper from chalcopyright isn't just an academic exercise,

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:21.000
it's becoming central to the energy transition. Researchers are now digging into the hidden surface

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:26.980
chemistry, the gatekeeper reactions that control how readily the mineral dissolves. A deeper understanding

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:31.980
of that surface behavior could open the door to better bioliching techniques, where especially

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:37.440
selected bacteria do the heavy lifting at ambient temperatures instead of relying on massive furnaces.

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:42.780
Bioliching has been studied for decades, but yields on chalcopyright have historically been low

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:48.120
precisely because of those stubborn surface reactions. If we can crack the gatekeeper chemistry,

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:53.320
we might be able to produce the copper we need with a much smaller energy and environmental footprint

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:58.600
without simply digging ever larger holes in the ground. The practical takeaway for me is that we

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:04.620
should pay closer attention to advances in mineralogy and biohydromedology. These fields don't usually

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:09.220
make headlines, but they could determine whether the clean tech future we're building is genuinely

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:15.740
sustainable or simply shifts environmental costs somewhere else. A single set of insights into

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:21.660
chalcopyright's surface behavior could have outsized impact. It's a reminder that solving climate-related

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:26.920
challenges often requires going back to fundamental materials, science, and geochemistry,

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:31.960
not just building more batteries or turbines. Before we go, keep an eye on how regulators

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:37.500
ultimately view that pancreatic cancer survival data and whether more of these liquid biopsy

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:43.380
platforms reach late-stage validation in the coming months. Both areas could shape how we treat tough

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:49.100
cancers and how we detect them earlier. That's Planet Terri and Daily for today. If you enjoyed the

